Recommendation: target a **10%** relative MDE. It is far below the effect this campaign type produced in the Criteo test,
so it is a conservative design target, and 5% needs {{ (Bf.rows[0].n_cupac / Bf.rows[1].n_cupac)|f(1) }}× the traffic. Whether an
effect of a given size *pays* depends on the cost per exposed user (break-even table in the [decision memo](decision_memo.md)). Run at least **14 days** in any case, to cover
two weekly cycles and the 14-day conversion window.

## Stopping rule

O'Brien–Fleming (Lan–DeMets) with four weekly looks at information fractions {{ Bf.looks|join(", ") }}. Stop for benefit
only if |z| exceeds {% for b in Bf.obf %}{{ b|f(2) }}{% if not loop.last %} / {% endif %}{% endfor %} at looks 1–4
(the last one is close to 1.96). Stop for harm at any look if a guardrail is significantly worse than its margin. The
dashboard's daily p-value is not a stopping rule: on real ASOS experiments, daily peeking flagged
{{ R.asos_portfolio.real_peeking.summary.naive.declared_significant }} of
{{ R.asos_portfolio.real_peeking.summary.n_trajectories }} metric trajectories, against
{{ R.asos_portfolio.real_peeking.summary.n_final_significant }} that were significant at the end.

## Decision rule (agreed before launch)

Ship (keep the campaign) if the primary metric is positive and significant after BH, all guardrails are within margin,
and the break-even condition holds at the CI lower bound: cost per exposed user < value per conversion × incremental
conversions per exposed user. HOLD on any trust-gate failure.
